---
description: Group related domain helpers under a namespace object (cargoInventory.transferLedgerByStop) instead of long flat names
alwaysApply: true
---

# Helper namespaces

When several functions share a domain (inventory, trip fixtures, packing, …), export a **namespace object** with short method names instead of long flat exports that repeat the domain in every name. Same idea for bay placement: `cargoBay.packAtStop` (not `planStopOccupancyAtStop`).

## Prefer

```ts
export const cargoInventory = {
  transferLedgerByStop(rows: HaulingChecklistRow[]): Map<string, StopTransferLedger> { ... },
  peakScuAtStop(ledger: StopTransferLedger): number { ... },
  peakStopId(rows: HaulingChecklistRow[]): string | null { ... },
};

// call sites
cargoInventory.transferLedgerByStop(stops);
cargoInventory.peakStopId(stops);
```

## Avoid

```ts
export function cargoBayInventoryByStop(...) { ... }
export function peakScuInStopInventory(...) { ... }
export function peakCargoBayStopId(...) { ... }
```

## Guidelines

1. **Noun object, verb methods** — `cargoInventory.transferLedgerByStop`, `cargoInventory.peakStopId` (not `cargoInventoryGetByStop`).
2. **Keep types as named exports** beside the object (`ScuTransfer`, `StopTransferLedger`) — do not bury types only inside the object unless they are truly private.
3. **Same file** — put the namespace in the domain module (`lib/missions/cargoInventory.ts`); call sites import `{ cargoInventory }`.
4. **Existing pattern** — same idea as `appApi` / `appPaths` for URL maps; use it for **behavior** helpers too when names would otherwise stack prefixes.
5. **Don’t force it** — a single standalone helper can stay a flat `export function`. Reach for a namespace when you have (or will have) a family of related operations.

Canonical example: `lib/missions/cargoInventory.ts` (`cargoInventory`).
